  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Gallen began producing women’s blouses and chose the invented name Ellen Tracy because he believed a womenswear label should carry a woman’s identity. He hired Linda Allard as a designer and gave her increasing creative authority. Together they extended the line from blouses into coordinated clothing for professional women.\n\nThe partnership endured for decades and helped the privately owned company build a reliable department-store following. Gallen supplied commercial control while Allard defined the aesthetic, creating a clear division between ownership and design. He eventually sold Ellen Tracy to Liz Claiborne, converting a long-built operating business into realised wealth. The company’s durability rested less on fashion spectacle than on consistent fit, quality and an understanding of working customers.",
    "html": "<p>Gallen began producing women’s blouses and chose the invented name Ellen Tracy because he believed a womenswear label should carry a woman’s identity. He hired Linda Allard as a designer and gave her increasing creative authority. Together they extended the line from blouses into coordinated clothing for professional women.</p>\r\n<p>The partnership endured for decades and helped the privately owned company build a reliable department-store following. Gallen supplied commercial control while Allard defined the aesthetic, creating a clear division between ownership and design. He eventually sold Ellen Tracy to Liz Claiborne, converting a long-built operating business into realised wealth. The company’s durability rested less on fashion spectacle than on consistent fit, quality and an understanding of working customers.</p>\r\n",
